WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:11.380
Directory traversal, cleanup, backup, log rotation, import, export, and recursive operations all need a symbolic-link policy.

2
00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:18.920
The code should define whether links are followed, ignored, reported, copied as link objects, or restricted by directory policy.

3
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:24.560
Recursive operations can easily cross the intended boundary if link behavior is unclear.

4
00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:29.620
A cleanup routine meant to remove files under one tree should preserve that tree's boundary.

5
00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:34.280
A backup routine should know whether it captures link objects or the objects they reference.

6
00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:38.220
A log rotation job should know what kind of entry it is rotating.

7
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:42.340
The policy should be documented before processing file trees.

8
00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:49.800
If a tool follows links in one mode but not another, tests and usage documentation should make that distinction clear.

9
00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:54.060
Hidden traversal behavior becomes hard to review and hard to operate safely.

10
00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:59.300
Cleanup deserves special attention because it modifies or removes filesystem entries.

11
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:06.440
The program should know whether it is deleting a link object, a file object, or a directory entry inside a trusted tree.

12
00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:11.180
It should avoid relying on ambiguous path state during recursive removal.

13
00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:21.780
Reviewers should trace traversal decisions from input path to directory entry handling, recursive descent, final operation, error handling, and cleanup.

14
00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:25.800
That makes it clear whether the operation remains inside the intended scope.
